--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` fills an omitted enum option from the case its declaration defaults to, by the same case name a command line would have typed — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`, `rule:enums/no-class-machinery`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A parameter default is folded at signature collection, and an enum case
// folds to its backing integer (`nvs_types::defaults`). A command row carries
// the default as *text* the matcher converts, so what it has to carry for an
// enum is the case's **name**: `nvs_types::commands::ArgConv::Enum` decided a
// command line spells a case by name, and a number no command line would be
// allowed to write is no use as the value one omitted.
//
// Both halves are here, because either alone prints plausibly: the omitted
// option takes `Warn`, and a written one still takes the word.
enum Level: uint {
    Warn = 10,
    Error = 20,
}

class Logs {
    #[Core\Command(name: "emit", about: "Write one line at a level")]
    public static function emit(#[Core\Option] Level $level = Level::Warn): uint {
        echo "level: ", $level as uint, "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

uint $status = Core\Command::run();
echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
--ARGS--
emit
--EXPECT--
level: 10
status: 0
